              "error_message": null
            }
          ],
          "error": null
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "text": "A cache eviction policy determines which items get discarded when the cache fills up, and a poor match between the policy and the actual access pattern causes frequently or soon-to-be-needed items to be evicted prematurely. When that happens, requests that would otherwise be fast cache hits become slow cache misses that must fall through to a backing store, database, or remote service. Because most requests still hit the cache and return quickly, the average latency may look fine, but the misses caused by bad eviction decisions cluster into the slow end of the distribution, inflating p95, p99, and other tail latency metrics. Additionally, mistimed evictions can trigger bursts of misses simultaneously (for example during traffic spikes or after a bulk invalidation), causing contention on the backing resource and further amplifying delays for the unlucky requests that miss, even though the system's typical behavior remains unaffected.",
